<DocsLayout
  title="Functions, Lambdas & Closures"
  description="Complete guide to Aion functions: expression bodies, default and named arguments, varargs and list spreading, lexical closures, overloading, and extension methods."
  category="Language Reference"
  currentSlug="functions"
  toc={toc}
  prevPage={{ slug: 'syntax', title: 'Syntax, Operators & Control Flow' }}
  nextPage={{ slug: 'types', title: 'Types, Generics & Interfaces' }}
>
  <h2 id="declaring-functions">Declaring Functions</h2>
  <p>
    Functions are declared with <code>fn</code>. Parameters always specify their type. With an expression body (<code>= expr</code>), the return type is inferred automatically; with a block body (<code>{'{'} ... {'}'}</code>), omit <code>-&gt; Type</code> if the function returns nothing (<code>Unit</code>), or specify <code>-&gt; Type</code> to return a value.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    title="functions.aion"
    code={`fn add(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int {
    return a + b
}

fn double(x: Int) = x * 2                       // one-liner; return type Int inferred
fn greet(name: String, greeting: String = "Hello") = "$greeting, $name!"

println(add(1, 2), double(21))
println(greet("Ann"))                           // default argument used
println(greet("Bob", greeting: "Hi"))           // named argument
println(greet(greeting: "Yo", name: "Cy"))      // named arguments in any order`}
    output={`3 42
Hello, Ann!
Hi, Bob!
Yo, Cy!`}
  />

  <ul>
    <li>After the first named argument in a call, all subsequent arguments must also be named. Arguments are evaluated in the order they are written at the call site.</li>
    <li>A recursive function (or pair of mutually recursive functions) requires an explicit <code>-&gt; ReturnType</code> on at least one of them.</li>
    <li><code>Unit</code> represents "no value" and is used in function type signatures such as <code>(Int) -&gt; Unit</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="defaults-and-named">Default Expressions, <code>vararg</code> &amp; Spread (<code>*xs</code>)</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Dynamic Defaults:</strong> Default parameter values are evaluated fresh on each call and can reference earlier parameters (or object fields inside methods). A default of <code>[]</code> creates a brand-new list on every call.</li>
    <li><strong>Variadic Parameters (<code>vararg</code>):</strong> The last parameter of a function, method, or custom <code>constructor</code> may be marked <code>vararg name: T</code>. Inside the body, it is a <code>List&lt;T&gt;</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>List Spread (<code>*xs</code>):</strong> Prefix a list with <code>*</code> to unpack its elements into a <code>vararg</code> call or into another list literal <code>[0, *xs, 40]</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <CodeBlock
    title="defaults_varargs.aion"
    code={`// Default parameters can use earlier parameters:
fn box(w: Int, h: Int = w, area: Int = w * h) = "$w x $h = $area"
println(box(3), "|", box(3, 4), "|", box(2, area: 7))

// Fresh list created on every call:
fn collect(item: Int, into: List<Int> = []) -> List<Int> {
    into.add(item)
    return into
}
println(collect(1), collect(2))

// vararg and * spread operator:
fn sum(vararg xs: Int) = xs.fold(0) { acc, x -> acc + x }
nums := [10, 20, 30]
println(sum(), sum(1, 2, 3), sum(*nums), sum(1, *nums))
println([0, *nums, 40])`}
    output={`3 x 3 = 9 | 3 x 4 = 12 | 2 x 2 = 7
[1] [2]
0 6 60 61
[0, 10, 20, 30, 40]`}
  />

  <h3 id="nested-functions">Nested &amp; Recursive Local Functions</h3>
  <p>
    Functions declared inside another function are closures: they can read and mutate local variables in their enclosing scope, call themselves recursively (when <code>-&gt; Type</code> is written), and be returned as values.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    title="nested_fn.aion"
    code={`fn counter() -> () -> Int {
    n := 0
    fn next() -> Int {
        n += 1
        return n
    }
    return next
}
c := counter()
c()
println(c())   // 2`}
    output={`2`}
  />

  <h2 id="lambdas-and-closures">Function Values, Lambdas &amp; Closures</h2>
  <p>
    Function types are written <code>(Param1, Param2) -&gt; ReturnType</code>. Lambdas are written <code>{'{'} params -&gt; body {'}'}</code>, where the last expression is the return value:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Implicit <code>it</code>:</strong> When a single-parameter lambda is passed where a function type is expected, you can omit <code>x -&gt;</code> and refer to the parameter as <code>it</code> (e.g. <code>xs.map {'{'} it * 2 {'}'}</code>).</li>
    <li><strong>Trailing Lambda Syntax:</strong> When the last argument of a call is a lambda, it can be placed outside the parentheses: <code>repeat(3) {'{'} println(it) {'}'}</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Shared Mutable Captures:</strong> If a captured local variable is reassigned anywhere (by the outer function or any closure), the compiler automatically boxes it into a single-slot heap cell so all closures share live mutations. Each loop iteration gets a fresh binding.</li>
  </ul>

  <CodeBlock
    title="closures.aion"
    code={`fn apply(f: (Int) -> Int, x: Int) = f(x)

println(apply({ x -> x * x }, 7))        // 49
println(apply({ it + 1 }, 41))           // 42 (implicit it)
println(apply({ x: Int -> x - 1 }, 10))  // 9  (explicit parameter type)

fn repeatTimes(n: Int, action: (Int) -> Unit) {
    for i in 0..n { action(i) }
}
repeatTimes(3) { print("step=$it ") }
println()`}
    output={`49
42
9
step=0 step=1 step=2`}
  />

  <h3 id="printing-functions">Printing Function Values &amp; Built-ins as Values</h3>
  <p>
    Function values can be printed directly, displaying their origin and signature. Built-in functions (<code>print</code>, <code>println</code>, <code>typeOf</code>, <code>hashOf</code>) can also be stored in variables or passed as <code>(Any) -&gt; ...</code> values:
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    title="fn_values.aion"
    code={`import math

fn add(a: Int, b: Int) = a + b
type Point(x: Int, y: Int)

println(add)                   // <function add(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int>
println({ x: Int -> x * 2 })   // <anonymous function (x: Int) -> Int>
println(Point)                 // <constructor Point(x: Int, y: Int) -> Point>
println(math.toRadians)        // <stdlib function math.toRadians(degrees: Float) -> Float>
println(println)               // <built-in function println(value: Any) -> Unit>`}
    output={`<function add(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int>
<anonymous function (x: Int) -> Int>
<constructor Point(x: Int, y: Int) -> Point>
<stdlib function math.toRadians(degrees: Float) -> Float>
<built-in function println(value: Any) -> Unit>`}
  />

  <h2 id="overloading">Function &amp; Method Overloading</h2>
  <p>
    Top-level functions, type methods, operator methods (such as <code>plus</code>), and extension methods can share a name as long as their parameter lists differ in arity or types. Exact type matches are preferred over widening conversions (e.g., <code>Int</code> beats <code>Float</code> for <code>describe(1)</code>).
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    title="overloading.aion"
    code={`fn describe(x: Int) = "int $x"
fn describe(x: Float) = "float $x"
fn describe(x: Int, y: Int) = "two ints \${x + y}"

type Money(cents: Int) {
    fn plus(o: Money) = Money(cents + o.cents)
    fn plus(extra: Int) = Money(cents + extra)
    fn toString() = "$$cents"
}

println(describe(1), "|", describe(2.5), "|", describe(3, 4))
println(Money(100) + Money(5), Money(100) + 7)`}
    output={`int 1 | float 2.5 | two ints 7
$105 $107`}
  />

  <Callout type="note" title="Overloading Rules">
    <code>extern fn</code>s, generic functions, and methods on <code>open</code> types or subtypes cannot be overloaded. To pass an overloaded function as a value, wrap the specific overload in a lambda: <code>{'{'} x: Int -&gt; describe(x) {'}'}</code>.
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="extension-methods">Extension Methods on Built-in &amp; Library Types</h2>
  <p>
    Any module can define methods on built-in types (<code>Int</code>, <code>Int32</code>, <code>Byte</code>, <code>Float</code>, <code>Bool</code>, <code>Char</code>, <code>String</code>, <code>Bytes</code>) as well as collection types (<code>List&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>Map&lt;K, V&gt;</code>, <code>Set&lt;T&gt;</code>) or user-defined types. Inside the method body, <code>this</code> refers to the receiver.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    title="extensions.aion"
    code={`fn String.shout() = "$this!"
fn Int.times(action: (Int) -> Unit) {
    for i in 0..this { action(i) }
}

println("hi".shout())
3.times { print("$it ") }
println()`}
    output={`hi!
0 1 2`}
  />

  <p>
    If your program declares an extension method with the same name and parameter types as a standard library method, your method takes precedence in your code while the standard library continues calling its own implementation internally.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
